---
title: Prepare your introduction video
description: A short script, recording tips and how to share your YouTube video as unlisted.
sidebar:
  order: 5.5
---

Your introduction video helps us verify your identity and see how you teach. Record **1 to 2 minutes**, with your face visible, and share the link in **step 2 of the course proposal form**.

You can prepare and save your proposal while you work on the video. The video field is optional.

## Script template

Use this outline as a starting point and adapt it to your experience. Speak naturally: you do not need to memorize a script or make a professional production.

1. **Introduce yourself (about 15 seconds).** “Hi, I'm [name or pseudonym]. I work in [area], and my experience with [topic] is [relevant experience].”
2. **Describe what you want to teach (about 20 seconds).** “I want to teach [topic] to [type of student], so they can [specific outcome].”
3. **Explain a concept (about 45 seconds).** Choose a simple idea from your course and explain it as if you were speaking to a student. Use an example or comparison to make it understandable.
4. **Wrap up (about 20 seconds).** Explain why you want to teach this topic and what students will be able to do after taking your course.

These timings are suggestions and add up to about 1 minute and 40 seconds. Rehearse once and adjust your content to stay between 1 and 2 minutes.

## Before recording

- **Lighting:** face a window or lamp that lights your face. Avoid a window or bright light behind you, which can leave your face in shadow.
- **Framing:** place the camera at eye level, keep your face and shoulders visible, and support your phone or camera so the image stays steady.
- **Audio:** choose a quiet room. Use an external microphone if available; your phone or computer microphone is also fine if your voice is clear.
- **Quality:** record in **landscape** at **720p or higher**.
- **Length:** keep the video between **1 and 2 minutes**.

Make a short test recording and play it back to check the lighting, framing and sound.

## Upload and share on YouTube

{% steps %}

1. Sign in to [YouTube Studio](https://studio.youtube.com/). Select **Create → Upload videos** and choose your file.
2. Complete the information YouTube asks for and continue to **Visibility**.
3. Select **Unlisted** and click **Save**.
4. Copy the video link. It can use the format `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=…` or `https://youtu.be/…`.
5. Open the link in an incognito window, without signing in, and check that the video plays and the audio is clear.
6. Paste the link into **Unlisted YouTube link**, in step 2 of [Propose a course](/en/profesores/crear-curso/).

{% /steps %}

If you already uploaded the video, open **YouTube Studio → Content**, find the video and change **Visibility → Unlisted → Save**. See [YouTube's visibility help](https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/157177?hl=en).

{% aside title="Who can watch the video" %}
**Anyone with the link can watch and share it.** An unlisted video is not published on your channel's Videos tab and normally does not appear in YouTube searches; it may appear if someone adds it to a public playlist. Viewers do not need a Google Account.

The link is also included in the course proposal document sent to GitHub for review. Avoid including personal information you do not want to share with people who can access that proposal.
{% /aside %}

## If the form rejects your link

Copy the link to a **specific video**, using `youtube.com/watch?v=…` or `youtu.be/…`. A link to your channel, a playlist or another platform does not work in this field.

The form validates the link's format. You check that the video is watchable by opening it without signing in.
